<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_reactive_control_state_machines" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction to Reactive Control and State Machines</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>In Chapter 3, we studied global path planning algorithms like A*, which calculate an entire path from start to goal before the robot ever turns its wheels. This global approach works well in static, fully known environments. However, real-world environments are dynamic and unpredictable: a human worker walks across a factory floor, a soccer ball rolls into a VEX competition lane, or a door closes unexpectedly.</p>
    <p><term>Reactive Navigation</term> focuses on rapid, local decision-making. Instead of storing a full map or computing complex paths, a reactive robot reads its real-time sensor data and immediately maps those inputs directly to actuator commands: sensors such as distance and IR readings feed into behavior logic built from if/elif/else rules, which in turn drives the actuators (drive motors) directly, with no map or planner in between.</p>
  </introduction>

  <figure align="center" xml:id="fig_reactive_control_flow">
    <caption>A reactive controller maps sensor readings directly through behavior rules to actuator commands.</caption>
    <image source="ch04_reactive_navigation_obstacle_avoidance/reactivestate.png" width="90%" alt="Reactive control flow from distance and infrared sensors through behavior logic to drive-motor actuators"/>
  </figure>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_reactive_applications">
    <title>Real-World &amp; VEX Robotics Applications</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><em class="emphasis">Real-World Example (Warehouse AGVs &amp; Vacuum Robots):</em> Automated Guided Vehicles (AGVs) in Amazon fulfillment centers use global planners to navigate between aisles. However, if a box falls off a shelf into the robot's path, a high-frequency reactive loop triggers an instant emergency stop or quick dodge without waiting for the central server to re-calculate a whole new map.</li>
        <li><em class="emphasis">VEX Robotics Example (Over Under / High Stakes Competitions):</em> During the autonomous period, a VEX robot driving toward a scoring zone might detect another robot blocking its path via its VEX Distance Sensor. A reactive behavior allows the VEX robot to pivot or back off instantly to avoid an illegal collision or entanglement penalty, regardless of its original pre-programmed trajectory.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_fsm_structure">
    <title>Structuring Reactive Logic: Finite State Machines (FSM)</title>
    <p>When writing reactive code, raw if/else statements can quickly turn into disorganized "spaghetti code." To prevent this, roboticists organize reactive control using a <term>Finite State Machine (FSM)</term>.</p>
    <figure align="center" xml:id="fig_finite_state_machine">
      <caption>A two-state finite state machine transitions between driving forward and turning left based on the measured obstacle distance.</caption>
      <image source="ch04_reactive_navigation_obstacle_avoidance/finite.png" width="75%" alt="Finite state machine transitioning between drive forward and turn left at a distance threshold of 0.5 meters"/>
    </figure>
    <p>An FSM breaks the robot's behavior into a set of discrete, mutually exclusive <term>states</term> (e.g., <c>DRIVE_FORWARD</c>, <c>TURN_LEFT</c>, <c>REVERSE</c>). The robot remains in its current state until a specific sensor reading triggers a <term>transition</term> to a new state.</p>
    <p>Consider a two-state FSM built from just <c>DRIVE_FORWARD</c> and <c>TURN_LEFT</c>. While in <c>DRIVE_FORWARD</c>, the robot keeps driving forward as long as the front distance reading stays at or above 0.5 meters; the moment that distance drops below 0.5 meters, it transitions to <c>TURN_LEFT</c>. While in <c>TURN_LEFT</c>, the robot keeps turning in place as long as the front distance stays below 0.5 meters; once the distance rises back to 0.5 meters or more, it transitions back to <c>DRIVE_FORWARD</c>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p><em class="emphasis">State 1: <c>DRIVE_FORWARD</c> (Default State)</em></p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><em class="emphasis">Action:</em> The robot drives straight, searching for an endpoint.</p></li>
          <li><p><em class="emphasis">Transition Trigger:</em> <c>Sensor value (distance &lt; threshold) = TRUE</c>.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p><em class="emphasis">If the condition is met, the system transitions to State 2.</em></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><em class="emphasis">State 2: <c>OBSTACLE_DETECTED</c> (The Reaction State)</em></p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><em class="emphasis">Action:</em> The robot immediately stops its wheels (a safety pause).</p></li>
          <li><p><em class="emphasis">Transition Trigger:</em> <c>State_2_Action_Completed = TRUE</c>.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p><em class="emphasis">A non-blocking state to quickly check the situation and decide the next move.</em></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><em class="emphasis">State 3: <c>TURN_LEFT</c> (Correction State)</em></p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><em class="emphasis">Action:</em> The robot executes a pivot (e.g., a 90-degree left turn).</p></li>
          <li><p><em class="emphasis">Transition Trigger:</em> <c>State_3_Action_Completed = TRUE</c>.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p><em class="emphasis">Once the rotation is complete, the robot returns to the Default State.</em></p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_fsm_cognitive_load">
    <title>Why FSMs Minimize Cognitive Load</title>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p><em class="emphasis">Isolation:</em> You only need to think about what the robot should do right now in its active state (e.g., while in <c>TURN_LEFT</c>, the motors are set to spin in place).</p></li>
        <li><p><em class="emphasis">Predictable Transitions:</em> State changes happen strictly based on explicit conditions (e.g., <c>distance &lt; 0.5m</c>), making troubleshooting simple during VEX lab testing.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_reactive_fsm_exercises">
    <title>Section 4.1 Interactive Exercises</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_fsm_state_switch_codelens">
      <title>Exercise 4.1.2: CodeLens Trace &#8212; Basic FSM State Switch</title>
      <p>Step through the execution of this FSM to watch how state switches occur dynamically as simulated sensor readings change.</p>
      <program xml:id="cl_fsm_trace" interactive="codelens" language="python">
        <input>
def run_fsm_step(state, front_sensor_dist):
    # State transition logic
    if state == "DRIVE_FORWARD":
        if front_sensor_dist &lt; 0.5:
            next_state = "TURN_LEFT"
            motor_command = (0.0, 0.5)  # Turn in place
        else:
            next_state = "DRIVE_FORWARD"
            motor_command = (0.5, 0.0)  # Forward motion

    elif state == "TURN_LEFT":
        if front_sensor_dist &gt;= 0.5:
            next_state = "DRIVE_FORWARD"
            motor_command = (0.5, 0.0)
        else:
            next_state = "TURN_LEFT"
            motor_command = (0.0, 0.5)

    return next_state, motor_command

# Trace loop over simulated sensor readings
sensor_readings = [1.2, 0.8, 0.3, 0.2, 0.6]
current_state = "DRIVE_FORWARD"

for dist in sensor_readings:
    current_state, cmd = run_fsm_step(current_state, dist)
    print("Dist:", dist, "| State:", current_state, "| Cmd:", cmd)
</input>
      </program>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <reading-questions>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <term>Check your understanding</term>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise label="ex_fsm_transition_check">
      <title>Exercise 4.1.1: FSM Transition Conceptual Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Why are reactive Finite State Machines (FSMs) commonly used alongside global path planners in dynamic, changing environments?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Global path planning algorithms calculate paths faster than FSMs.</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Global planners are computationally heavier and take longer to re-compute than simple state transitions.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Reactive FSMs respond immediately to immediate sensor changes without needing a full pre-computed map.</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct! Reactive state machines allow robots to immediately react to unpredictable local changes (like moving obstacles) without needing a pre-built map.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>FSMs remember the exact 3D shape of every obstacle they encounter.</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. FSMs are memoryless reactive loops; they do not construct 3D maps.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>FSMs do not require any sensors to make decisions.</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Reactive control relies heavily on real-time sensor feedback.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </reading-questions>
</section>
